order: 22
---
short_label: X.22
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: lemma
----
statement: If there are two straight lines, then the first is to the second as the square on the first is to the rectangle contained by the two straight lines.
----
proof:

If there are two straight lines, then the first is to the second as the square on the first is to the rectangle contained by the two straight lines.

Let {FE} and {EG} be two straight lines.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="240" height="160" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.22 lemma diagram" src="propX22l.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's lemma figure: the square FD on FE (random face) and the
    // rectangle DG beside it (default opaque face). The given lines FE and
    // EG are drawn only as edges of those two, so solid twins FE / EG
    // (initiallyHidden — the resting figure is unchanged) carry them on the
    // slides before the square is described. DE is a transparent highlight
    // target (;0;0;0). The square and the rectangle are lit through
    // faceless, strokeless twins FDh / DGh (#140); prose and captions reach
    // them with the display override ({DF|FDh}, {GD|DGh}).
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): F, E, G (given) and the
    // offscreen frees. D is a derived vertex, listed from "Describe".
    var L_GIVEN = ["F","E","G","FE","EG"];
    var L_SQ    = L_GIVEN.concat(["FD","FDh","D","DG","DGh","DE"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "Lemma",
        elements: [
            "F0;point;free;-1000,40;0;0",
            "F3;point;free;1000,40;0;0",
            "F;point;lineSlider;F0,F3,40,0",
            "G;point;lineSlider;F0,F3,200,0",
            "E;point;lineSlider;F0,F3,110,0",
            "FD;polygon;square;E,F;0;0;black;random",
            "D;point;vertex;FD,4",
            "DG;polygon;parallelogram;D,E,G",
            // Slide-only twins and highlight targets.
            "FE;line;connect;F,E",
            "EG;line;connect;E,G",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            "FDh;polygon;square;E,F;0;0;0;0",
            "DGh;polygon;parallelogram;D,E,G;0;0;0;0"
        ],
        initiallyHidden: ["FE","EG"],
        slides: [
            { text: "“If there are two straight lines, then the first is to the second",
              visible: L_GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["FE","EG"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Lemma" }] },
            { text: "as the square on the first is to the rectangle contained by the two straight lines.”",
              visible: L_GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["FE","EG"] },
            { text: "Let {FE} and {EG} be two straight lines.",
              visible: L_GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["FE","EG"] },
            { text: "I say that {FE} is to {EG} as the square on {FE} is to the rectangle {FE} by {EG}.",
              visible: L_GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["FE","EG"] },
            { text: "Describe the square {DF|FDh} on {FE}, and complete {GD|DGh}.",
              visible: L_SQ,
              highlighted: ["FE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "the square on FE", ref: "I.46" }],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "FDh", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline },
                  { elem: "DGh", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] } },
            { text: "Since then {FE} is to {EG} as {FD|FDh} is to {DG|DGh},",
              visible: L_SQ,
              highlighted: ["FE","EG","FDh","DGh"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "FE : EG = FD : DG", ref: "VI.1" }] },
            { text: "and {FD|FDh} is the square on {FE}, and {DG|DGh} the rectangle {DE} by {EG}, that is, the rectangle {FE} by {EG},",
              visible: L_SQ,
              highlighted: ["FDh","DGh","DE","EG","FE"] },
            { text: "therefore {FE} is to {EG} as the square on {FE} is to the rectangle {FE} by {EG}.",
              visible: L_SQ,
              highlighted: ["FE","EG","FDh","DGh"] },
            { text: "Similarly the rectangle {GE|EG} by {EF|FE} is to the square on {EF|FE},",
              visible: L_SQ,
              highlighted: ["DGh","FDh"] },
            { text: "that is {GD|DGh} is to {FD|FDh}, as {GE|EG} is to {EF|FE}.",
              visible: L_SQ,
              highlighted: ["DGh","FDh","EG","FE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "GD : FD = GE : EF", ref: "VI.1" }, { ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "Lemma") return "#lemma";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification
    // (shared by both canvases on this page).
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.46": "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI46/",
        "VI.1": "/elements/books/bookVI/propositions/propVI1/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say that {FE} is to {EG} as the square on {FE} is to the rectangle {FE} by {EG}.

Describe the square {DF|FDh} on {FE}, and complete {GD|DGh}.

Since then {FE} is to {EG} as {FD|FDh} is to {DG|DGh}, and {FD|FDh} is the square on {FE}, and {DG|DGh} the rectangle {DE} by {EG}, that is, the rectangle {FE} by {EG}, therefore {FE} is to {EG} as the square on {FE} is to the rectangle {FE} by {EG}. Similarly the rectangle {GE|EG} by {EF|FE} is to the square on {EF|FE}, that is {GD|DGh} is to {FD|FDh}, as {GE|EG} is to {EF|FE}. [!just VI.1]

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">
#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: The square on a medial straight line, if applied to a rational straight line, produces as breadth a straight line rational and incommensurable in length with that to which it is applied.
----
proof:

The square on a medial straight line, if applied to a rational straight line, produces as breadth a straight line rational and incommensurable in length with that to which it is applied.

Let {A:canvas_1} be medial and {CB|BC:canvas_1} rational, and let a rectangular area {BD|BDh:canvas_1} equal to the square on {A:canvas_1} be applied to {BC:canvas_1}, producing {CD:canvas_1} as breadth.

I say that {CD:canvas_1} is rational and incommensurable in length with {CB|BC:canvas_1}.

Since {A:canvas_1} is medial, the square on it equals a rectangular area contained by rational straight lines commensurable in square only. [!just X.21]

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="270" height="250" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.22 diagram" src="propX22.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure: the medial line A, the rectangle BD applied to BC
    // with breadth CD, and the rectangle GF (sides EG, EF) equal to the
    // square on A. Added, all invisible at rest: transparent highlight
    // targets CD and EF (;0;0;0); faceless, strokeless twins BDh / FGh that
    // carry the rectangles' highlights so a raised random face never covers
    // the figure (#140); right-angle markers at C and E for "equiangular"
    // (hidden at rest, showAngles is off). The prose above this canvas
    // binds to it explicitly ({A:canvas_1}); captions use the display
    // override for Joyce's spellings ({CB|BC}, {DC|CD}, {FE|EF}, {GF|FGh}).
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A1, A2, B, C (given), E, G,
    // and the offscreen frees. E and G only arrive with "Let the square on
    // it equal GF", so they are deferred; D and F are derived and listed.
    var GIVEN = ["A","A1","A2","B","C","BC","D","BD","BDh","CD"];
    var GF    = GIVEN.concat(["E","G","EG","F","EF","FG","FGh"]);
    var ANG   = GF.concat(["angC","angE"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.22",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;40,-1000;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;40,1000;0;0",
            "B0;point;free;75,-1000;0;0",
            "B3;point;free;75,1000;0;0",
            "E0;point;free;160,-1000;0;0",
            "E3;point;free;160,1000;0;0",
            "A1;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,0,40;0",
            "A2;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,0,130;0",
            "A;line;connect;A1,A2;black",
            "B;point;lineSlider;B0,B3,0,40",
            "C;point;lineSlider;B0,B3,0,200",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "D';point;perpendicular;C,B;0;0",
            "D'';point;extend;D',C,D',C;0;0",
            "D;point;proportion;B,C,A1,A2,A1,A2,C,D''",
            "BD;polygon;parallelogram;B,C,D;0;0;black;random",
            "G;point;lineSlider;E0,E3,0,50",
            "E;point;lineSlider;E0,E3,0,160",
            "EG;line;connect;E,G",
            "F';point;perpendicular;E,G;0;0",
            "F'';point;extend;F',E,F',E;0;0",
            "F;point;proportion;E,G,A1,A2,A1,A2,E,F''",
            "FG;polygon;parallelogram;F,E,G;0;0;black;random",
            // Highlight targets and markers only — invisible at rest.
            "CD;line;connect;C,D;0;0;0",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F;0;0;0",
            "BDh;polygon;parallelogram;B,C,D;0;0;0;0",
            "FGh;polygon;parallelogram;F,E,G;0;0;0;0",
            "angC;sector;angleMarkerRight;C,B,D",
            "angE;sector;angleMarkerRight;E,G,F"
        ],
        deferDraggables: ["E","G"],
        slides: [
            { text: "“The square on a medial straight line, if applied to a rational straight line,",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["A","BC"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "X.22" }] },
            { text: "produces as breadth a straight line rational and incommensurable in length with that to which it is applied.”",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["CD","BC"] },
            { text: "Let {A} be medial and {CB|BC} rational,",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["A","BC"] },
            { text: "and let a rectangular area {BD|BDh} equal to the square on {A} be applied to {BC}, producing {CD} as breadth.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["BDh","A","BC","CD"] },
            { text: "I say that {CD} is rational and incommensurable in length with {CB|BC}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["CD","BC"] },
            { text: "Since {A} is medial,",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["A"] },
            { text: "the square on it equals a rectangular area contained by rational straight lines commensurable in square only.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["A"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "square on a medial line", ref: "X.21" }] },
            { text: "Let the square on it equal {GF|FGh}.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["FGh","A"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "FGh", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] } },
            { text: "But the square on it also equals {BD|BDh}, therefore {BD|BDh} equals {GF|FGh}.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["BDh","FGh"] },
            { text: "But it is also equiangular with it,",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["BDh","FGh"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "and in equal and equiangular parallelograms the sides about the equal angles are reciprocally proportional,",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["BC","CD","EG","EF"] },
            { text: "therefore, {BC} is to {EG} as {EF} is to {CD}.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["BC","EG","EF","CD"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "BC : EG = EF : CD", ref: "VI.14" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the square on {BC} is to the square on {EG} as the square on {EF} is to the square on {CD}.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["BC","EG","EF","CD"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "BC² : EG² = EF² : CD²", ref: "VI.22" }] },
            { text: "But the square on {CB|BC} is commensurable with the square on {EG}, for each of these straight lines is rational,",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["BC","EG"] },
            { text: "therefore the square on {EF} is also commensurable with the square on {CD}.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["EF","CD"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "EF², CD² commensurable", ref: "X.11" }] },
            { text: "But the square on {EF} is rational, therefore the square on {CD} is also rational. Therefore {CD} is rational.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["EF","CD"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "CD rational", ref: "X.Def.I.4" }] },
            { text: "And since {EF} is incommensurable in length with {EG}, for they are commensurable in square only,",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["EF","EG"] },
            { text: "while {EF} is to {EG} as the square on {EF} is to the rectangle {FE|EF} by {EG},",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["EF","EG","FGh"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "EF : EG = EF² : EF·EG", ref: "Lemma" }] },
            { text: "therefore the square on {EF} is incommensurable with the rectangle {FE|EF} by {EG}.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["EF","FGh"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "EF², EF·EG incommensurable", ref: "X.11" }] },
            { text: "But the square on {CD} is commensurable with the square on {EF}, for the straight lines are rational in square,",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["CD","EF"] },
            { text: "and the rectangle {DC|CD} by {CB|BC} is commensurable with the rectangle {FE|EF} by {EG}, for they equal the square on {A},",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["BDh","FGh","A"] },
            { text: "therefore the square on {CD} is incommensurable with the rectangle {DC|CD} by {CB|BC}.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["CD","BDh"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "CD², DC·CB incommensurable", ref: "X.13" }] },
            { text: "But the square on {CD} is to the rectangle {DC|CD} by {CB|BC} as {DC|CD} is to {CB|BC},",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["CD","BC","BDh"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "CD² : DC·CB = DC : CB", ref: "Lemma" }] },
            { text: "therefore {DC|CD} is incommensurable in length with {CB|BC}.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["CD","BC"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "DC, CB incommensurable in length", ref: "X.11" }] },
            { text: "Therefore {CD} is rational and incommensurable in length with {CB|BC}.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["CD","BC"] },
            { text: "Therefore, the square on a medial straight line, if applied to a rational straight line,",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["A","BC"] },
            { text: "produces as breadth a straight line rational and incommensurable in length with that to which it is applied.",
              visible: GF,
              highlighted: ["CD","BC"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "X.22") return "#prop";
            if (ref === "Lemma") return "#lemma";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "VI.14":     "/elements/books/bookVI/propositions/propVI14/",
        "VI.22":     "/elements/books/bookVI/propositions/propVI22/",
        "X.11":      "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX11/",
        "X.13":      "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX13/",
        "X.21":      "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX21/",
        "X.Def.I.4": "/elements/books/bookX/definitions/defX.I.4/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Let the square on it equal {GF|FGh}. But the square on it also equals {BD|BDh}, therefore {BD|BDh} equals {GF|FGh}.

But it is also equiangular with it, and in equal and equiangular parallelograms the sides about the equal angles are reciprocally proportional, therefore, {BC} is to {EG} as {EF} is to {CD}. [!just VI.14]

Therefore the square on {BC} is to the square on {EG} as the square on {EF} is to the square on {CD}. [!just VI.22]

But the square on {CB|BC} is commensurable with the square on {EG}, for each of these straight lines is rational, therefore the square on {EF} is also commensurable with the square on {CD}. [!just X.11]

But the square on {EF} is rational, therefore the square on {CD} is also rational. Therefore {CD} is rational. [!just X.Def.I.4]

And since {EF} is incommensurable in length with {EG}, for they are commensurable in square only, while {EF} is to {EG} as the square on {EF} is to the rectangle {FE|EF} by {EG}, therefore the square on {EF} is incommensurable with the rectangle {FE|EF} by {EG}. <div class="just"><a href="#lemma">Lemma</a><br><a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX11/">X.11</a></div>

But the square on {CD} is commensurable with the square on {EF}, for the straight lines are rational in square, and the rectangle {DC|CD} by {CB|BC} is commensurable with the rectangle {FE|EF} by {EG}, for they equal the square on {A}, therefore the square on {CD} is incommensurable with the rectangle {DC|CD} by {CB|BC}. [!just X.13]

But the square on {CD} is to the rectangle {DC|CD} by {CB|BC} as {DC|CD} is to {CB|BC}, therefore {DC|CD} is incommensurable in length with {CB|BC}. <div class="just"><a href="#lemma">Lemma</a>, <a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX11/">X.11</a></div>

Therefore {CD} is rational and incommensurable in length with {CB|BC}.

Therefore, *the square on a medial straight line, if applied to a rational straight line, produces as breadth a straight line rational and incommensurable in length with that to which it is applied.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

Given medial line {A} and rational line {BC}, then {CD} = {A}²/({BC}) is a rational line incommensurable to {BC}.

To understand this, let *a* be the length of {A} (relative to the standard unit length), *b* the length of {BC}, and *c* the length of {CD}, so that *c* = *a*²/*b*.

We're given a medial number *a* (*a*⁴ is a rational number but *a*² is an irrational number), and *b*² is a rational number. Then it follows that *c*² is a rational number (being *a*⁴/*b*²), but *c*/*b* is an irrational number (being *a*²/*b*²). That follows since the product or quotient of two rational numbers is a rational number, but the product or quotient of an irrational number and a rational number is an irrational number.

This proposition is used frequently in Book X starting with the next proposition.
